th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","history_only":true,"curation_sha256":"402117cd2ecc28eaf6387aaefcadec5f0c0540f0912f8a96983845daf37ec8e2","concept_id":"Year-2/2b-Risk-Insurance/risk-orders-under-aggregation.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-2/2b-Risk-Insurance/risk-orders-under-aggregation.html","questions":[{"id":"year_2_2b_risk_insurance_risk_orders_under_aggregation_99697398_007","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For an integrable non-negative loss X, let $\\pi_X(d)=E[(X-d)_+]$ and form the aggregate $S=X+3$. Which expression for $E[(S-5)_+]$ is valid for every integrable non-negative law of X?","options":["$\\pi_X(2)$.","$\\pi_X(5)+3$.","$\\pi_X(8)$.","$\\pi_X(5)-3$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"48a881236fefe90ff87c2c0f","time_limit":25,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"no_diagnostic_value"},{"id":"year_2_2b_risk_insurance_risk_orders_under_aggregation_99697398_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"A count N is Bernoulli with probability $1/2$, independent of iid severities that are zero or two with equal probabilities. Let $S=\\sum_{i=1}^N X_i$. What is $P(S=0)$?","options":["$1/2$.","$1/4$.","$3/4$.","$0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3b4f50386952e88a5da83beb","time_limit":25,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"no_diagnostic_value"},{"id":"year_2_2b_risk_insurance_risk_orders_under_aggregation_99697398_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"A policy loss is IX, with I Bernoulli probability 0.3 independent of non-negative integrable severity X. Another portfolio has iid copies of X independent of count N, where $\\mathbb{P}(N=2)=0.2$ and $\\mathbb{P}(N=0)=0.8$. Which check justifies $IX\\le_{\\mathrm{sl}}\\sum_{i=1}^NX_i$?","options":["The probability of any claim0.2 is below0.3, so the bound fails.","The count mean 0.4 exceeds the policy probability 0.3.","The zero-count probability0.8 exceeds0.3, so it establishes the bound.","The count must be at least one on every outcome."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d88c1f7419dcb88f8db44508","time_limit":35,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_2_2b_risk_insurance_risk_orders_under_aggregation_99697398_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Two usual independent iid non-negative random-sum models have ordered severities and counts in stop-loss order. Their finite count means are two and three, and their severity means are three and four, respectively. What aggregate mean comparison is consistent with preservation?","options":["$5\\le7$.","$9\\le8$.","$3\\le4$.","$6\\le12$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b679e6db7afc8fe21bb7fb15","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"no_diagnostic_value"},{"id":"year_2_2b_risk_insurance_risk_orders_under_aggregation_99697398_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"A portfolio S sums two independent Bernoulli probability 1/2 losses. A second total T sums two certain losses of size two. Corresponding summands and hence totals are in stop-loss order S below T. Does this aggregate order force $\\operatorname{Var}(S)\\le\\operatorname{Var}(T)$?","options":["Yes; the variances are zero and 0.5 by aggregate order.","Yes; both variances are 0.5 because there are two summands.","No; both variances are zero because the means are finite.","No; the variances are 0.5 and zero, while the means differ."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8e0b9b27d695ac729f0eab45","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"no_diagnostic_value"}]}}